QUESTIONS = [
    # CH52 GST Council L1 (+29)
    {"text":"Under which Article of the Constitution is the Goods and Services Tax (GST) Council established?","options":["Article 268A","Article 269A","Article 279A","Article 280"],"correct_answer":"Article 279A","explanation":"The GST Council is a constitutional body established under Article 279A of the Indian Constitution, inserted by the 101st Amendment Act, 2016.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which Constitutional Amendment Act paved the way for the introduction of Goods and Services Tax (GST) and the GST Council?","options":["100th Amendment Act, 2015","101st Amendment Act, 2016","102nd Amendment Act, 2018","103rd Amendment Act, 2019"],"correct_answer":"101st Amendment Act, 2016","explanation":"The Constitution (One Hundred and First Amendment) Act, 2016 introduced a national Goods and Services Tax (GST) and authorized the creation of the GST Council.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who empowers the constitution of the GST Council?","options":["The Parliament","The Prime Minister","The President of India","The Supreme Court"],"correct_answer":"The President of India","explanation":"Article 279A(1) empowers the President of India to constitute the GST Council within 60 days of the commencement of the 101st Amendment Act.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Where is the Secretariat of the GST Council located?","options":["Mumbai","Chennai","New Delhi","Kolkata"],"correct_answer":"New Delhi","explanation":"The official Secretariat of the GST Council is located in New Delhi. The Union Revenue Secretary acts as the ex-officio Secretary to the GST Council.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who serves as the Chairperson of the GST Council?","options":["The Prime Minister of India","The Governor of the Reserve Bank of India","The Union Finance Minister","The Comptroller and Auditor General"],"correct_answer":"The Union Finance Minister","explanation":"According to Article 279A(2)(a), the Union Finance Minister is the Chairperson of the GST Council.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Besides the Chairperson, who represents the Central Government in the GST Council?","options":["The Commerce Minister","The Union Minister of State in charge of Revenue or Finance","The Chief Economic Advisor","The Cabinet Secretary"],"correct_answer":"The Union Minister of State in charge of Revenue or Finance","explanation":"Article 279A(2)(b) states that the Union Minister of State in charge of Revenue or Finance represents the Centre in the Council alongside the Chairperson.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who represents the states in the GST Council?","options":["The Chief Ministers of all states","The State Governors","The Minister in charge of Finance or Taxation or any other Minister nominated by each State Government","The Finance Secretaries of all states"],"correct_answer":"The Minister in charge of Finance or Taxation or any other Minister nominated by each State Government","explanation":"Article 279A(2)(c) provides that each State Government must nominate its Minister in charge of Finance or Taxation, or any other Minister, as a member of the GST Council.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who decides the Vice-Chairperson of the GST Council?","options":["The President of India appoints them","The Chairperson nominates them","The State government representatives choose one amongst themselves","The Prime Minister selects them from the Union Cabinet"],"correct_answer":"The State government representatives choose one amongst themselves","explanation":"Article 279A(3) dictates that the Members of the GST Council (specifically the state ministers) shall choose one amongst themselves to be the Vice-Chairperson of the Council for such period as they may decide.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"What is the basic function of the GST Council?","options":["To regulate foreign direct investment","To make recommendations on issues related to GST to the Union and State governments","To adjudicate inter-state water disputes","To implement the annual Union Budget"],"correct_answer":"To make recommendations on issues related to GST to the Union and State governments","explanation":"The GST Council is a joint forum of the Centre and the States designed to make recommendations to both on various aspects of GST, such as tax rates, exemptions, and dispute resolution.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following does the GST Council NOT recommend?","options":["The taxes, cesses, and surcharges to be subsumed in GST","The goods and services that may be subjected to or exempted from GST","The appointment of the RBI Governor","The model GST Laws"],"correct_answer":"The appointment of the RBI Governor","explanation":"The GST Council's mandate is strictly related to indirect taxation (GST structure, rates, exemptions). It has no role in appointing the RBI Governor.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Does the GST Council have the power to recommend the threshold limit of turnover for GST exemption?","options":["Yes, it recommends the threshold limit below which goods and services may be exempted","No, Parliament arbitrarily decides without the Council","No, it is fixed by the Finance Commission","Only the State Legislatures can decide this"],"correct_answer":"Yes, it recommends the threshold limit below which goods and services may be exempted","explanation":"Article 279A(4)(d) specifically empowers the GST Council to make recommendations on the threshold limit of turnover below which goods and services may be exempted from tax.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"What is the quorum requirement for holding a meeting of the GST Council?","options":["One-third of the total number of members","One-half of the total number of members","Two-thirds of the total number of members","Three-fourths of the total number of members"],"correct_answer":"One-half of the total number of members","explanation":"Article 279A(7) states that one-half (50%) of the total number of Members of the Goods and Services Tax Council shall constitute the quorum for its meetings.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Decisions of the GST Council are taken by a majority of not less than what proportion of the weighted votes of members present and voting?","options":["Simple majority (more than 50%)","Two-thirds (66.6%)","Three-fourths (75%)","Unanimous consensus (100%)"],"correct_answer":"Three-fourths (75%)","explanation":"Article 279A(9) mandates that every decision of the GST Council must be taken by a majority of not less than three-fourths (75%) of the weighted votes of the members present and voting.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"In the GST Council voting structure, what is the weightage of the vote of the Central Government?","options":["One-fourth of the total votes cast","One-third of the total votes cast","One-half of the total votes cast","Two-thirds of the total votes cast"],"correct_answer":"One-third of the total votes cast","explanation":"The vote of the Central Government has a weightage of one-third (33.33%) of the total votes cast in that meeting.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"In the GST Council voting structure, what is the combined weightage of the votes of all the State Governments?","options":["One-half of the total votes cast","One-third of the total votes cast","Two-thirds of the total votes cast","Three-fourths of the total votes cast"],"correct_answer":"Two-thirds of the total votes cast","explanation":"The votes of all the State Governments combined shall have a weightage of two-thirds (66.67%) of the total votes cast in that meeting.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Can the GST Council establish a mechanism to adjudicate disputes arising out of its recommendations?","options":["Yes, it is mandated to establish a mechanism for dispute resolution","No, the Supreme Court handles all disputes exclusively","No, the Inter-State Council handles all such disputes","Yes, but only if Parliament passes a specific law for it"],"correct_answer":"Yes, it is mandated to establish a mechanism for dispute resolution","explanation":"Article 279A(11) states that the GST Council shall establish a mechanism to adjudicate any dispute arising out of its recommendations.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"The GST Council is a joint forum of:","options":["The Centre and the NITI Aayog","The Centre and the States","The States and the Finance Commission","The Parliament and the Supreme Court"],"correct_answer":"The Centre and the States","explanation":"The GST Council is expressly established as a joint forum of the Centre and the States, embodying the principle of cooperative federalism.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Are Union Territories represented in the GST Council?","options":["No, UTs have no representation","Only UTs with a legislature are treated as 'States' and are represented","All UTs are directly represented by the Home Minister","Only Delhi and Puducherry are represented by special invitees"],"correct_answer":"Only UTs with a legislature are treated as 'States' and are represented","explanation":"In the context of the GST Council, Union Territories with a legislature (like Delhi, Puducherry, and later J&K) are considered 'States' and their finance ministers represent them in the Council.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who acts as the ex-officio Secretary to the GST Council?","options":["The Cabinet Secretary","The Chairperson of the Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs (CBIC)","The Revenue Secretary, Government of India","The Chief Economic Advisor"],"correct_answer":"The Revenue Secretary, Government of India","explanation":"The Union Revenue Secretary acts as the ex-officio Secretary to the GST Council Secretariat.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Is the Chairperson of the Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs (CBIC) a voting member of the GST Council?","options":["Yes, with equal voting rights as a state minister","Yes, but with a reduced voting right","No, they are a permanent invitee with no voting rights","No, they are completely excluded from the meetings"],"correct_answer":"No, they are a permanent invitee with no voting rights","explanation":"The Chairperson of the CBIC acts as a permanent invitee to all proceedings of the GST Council, but does NOT possess a voting right.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Does an action of the GST Council become invalid if there is a vacancy in its membership?","options":["Yes, proceedings freeze until the vacancy is filled","No, under Article 279A(10), no act is invalid merely due to a vacancy or defect in its constitution","Yes, if the vacancy is from the Union government side","No, unless the Supreme Court intervenes"],"correct_answer":"No, under Article 279A(10), no act is invalid merely due to a vacancy or defect in its constitution","explanation":"Article 279A(10) validates the proceedings of the GST Council by stating that no act or proceeding shall be invalid merely by reason of any vacancy in, or defect in the constitution of, the Council.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"A major objective of the GST Council is to develop a harmonized national market for goods and services. What principle guides this?","options":["Competitive Autocracy","Centralized Command Economy","Cooperative Federalism","Laissez-Faire Capitalism"],"correct_answer":"Cooperative Federalism","explanation":"The design of the GST Council, acting as a joint forum of the Centre and the States requiring consensus, embodies the principle of cooperative federalism.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following items was explicitly left out of the immediate application of GST, with the GST Council given the power to decide the date from which GST will apply to it?","options":["Gold and Jewellery","Alcoholic liquor for human consumption","Petroleum crude, high speed diesel, motor spirit, natural gas, and aviation turbine fuel","Tobacco products"],"correct_answer":"Petroleum crude, high speed diesel, motor spirit, natural gas, and aviation turbine fuel","explanation":"Article 279A(5) specifically states that the GST Council shall recommend the date on which GST shall be levied on petroleum crude, high speed diesel, motor spirit (petrol), natural gas, and aviation turbine fuel. (Note: Alcoholic liquor for human consumption is constitutionally excluded).","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"What provision allows the GST Council to help states in times of natural calamities?","options":["It can impose President's Rule","It can declare a financial emergency","It can recommend special rates for a specified period to raise additional resources during any natural calamity or disaster","It can borrow unrestricted funds from the IMF"],"correct_answer":"It can recommend special rates for a specified period to raise additional resources during any natural calamity or disaster","explanation":"Article 279A(4)(f) empowers the GST Council to recommend any special rate or rates for a specified period to raise additional resources during any natural calamity or disaster. (e.g., the Kerala flood cess).","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which regions has the GST Council been constitutionally directed to recommend 'special provisions' for?","options":["Coastal States","Special Category States like North-Eastern States, Himalayan States (J&K, HP, Uttarakhand)","Left-wing Extremism affected areas","Union Territories without legislatures"],"correct_answer":"Special Category States like North-Eastern States, Himalayan States (J&K, HP, Uttarakhand)","explanation":"Article 279A(4)(g) mandates the Council to make special provisions with respect to Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, J&K, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, Sikkim, Tripura, HP, and Uttarakhand.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"With respect to what aspect of 'taxes, cesses and surcharges' does the GST Council make recommendations?","options":["Raising corporate tax rates to fund GST shortfalls","Which Union and State taxes, cesses, and surcharges should be subsumed in the GST","Levying new agricultural cesses separate from GST","Abolishing the Income Tax altogether"],"correct_answer":"Which Union and State taxes, cesses, and surcharges should be subsumed in the GST","explanation":"Article 279A(4)(a) mandates the GST Council to recommend the taxes, cesses and surcharges levied by the Union, States, and local bodies which may be subsumed in the goods and services tax.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Can the GST Council recommend 'floor rates with bands' of GST?","options":["No, only uniform absolute rates are allowed","Yes, Article 279A(4)(e) allows the recommendation of rates including floor rates with bands","Yes, but only for the Central GST, not State GST","No, this power lies with the Finance Commission"],"correct_answer":"Yes, Article 279A(4)(e) allows the recommendation of rates including floor rates with bands","explanation":"The Constitution explicitly empowers the GST Council to recommend the rates of goods and services tax, which includes recommending 'floor rates with bands' to allow slight variations by states if needed.","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"In addition to specified matters, can the GST Council advise on other GST-related issues?","options":["No, its mandate is exhaustively defined and closed","Yes, it can recommend any other matter relating to GST, as the Council may decide","Yes, but only if the Supreme Court requests it","Only on matters explicitly approved by the Finance Commission"],"correct_answer":"Yes, it can recommend any other matter relating to GST, as the Council may decide","explanation":"Under Article 279A(4)(h), the GST Council has a residuary power to make recommendations on 'any other matter relating to the goods and services tax, as the Council may decide.'","chapter_number":52,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which schedule of the Constitution deals with the allocation of seats in Rajya Sabha, completely unrelated to GST?","options":["Seventh Schedule","Eighth Schedule","Ninth Schedule","Fourth Schedule"],"correct_answer":"Fourth Schedule","explanation":"While GST involved altering the Seventh Schedule (Union/State lists), it has no relation to the Fourth Schedule (allocation of Rajya Sabha seats).","chapter_number":52,"level":1},

    # CH52 GST Council L2 (+21)
    {"text":"Consider the following regarding the voting weightage in the GST Council:\n1. For a resolution to pass, it needs 75% of the weighted votes of members present and voting.\n2. The Centre's vote has a weightage of exactly 33.33%.\n3. This structure means neither the Centre alone nor the States collectively can pass a decision without the other's agreement.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. Since the Centre has 33.33% and states together have 66.67%, and a decision needs 75% to pass, the Centre essentially holds a veto (as states max out at 66.67%). Conversely, the Centre cannot pass a decision without the support of a significant number of states (as it only has 33.33%).","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the mechanism for dispute adjudication by the GST Council (Article 279A(11)), the Council can adjudicate disputes between:\n1. The Government of India and one or more States.\n2. The Government of India and any State or States on one side, and one or more other States on the other side.\n3. Two or more States.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"Article 279A(11) specifically mandates the GST Council to establish a mechanism to adjudicate disputes arising out of its recommendations between the Centre and states, or between various states, covering all three mentioned scenarios.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the nature of the GST Council's recommendations:\n1. The Supreme Court has ruled that the GST Council's recommendations are non-binding and possess persuasive value.\n2. Both Parliament and State legislatures have simultaneous power to legislate on GST (Article 246A).\n3. Any deviation by a State from a GST Council recommendation automatically renders the state's GST law unconstitutional.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. In Union of India vs Mohit Minerals (2022), the Supreme Court clarified that the GST Council's recommendations are not binding edicts; they have persuasive value. Since Article 246A grants simultaneous legislative power, state deviations are not automatically unconstitutional (though they disrupt tax harmony). Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the position of 'Alcoholic liquor for human consumption' under GST:\n1. It is currently outside the purview of the GST regime.\n2. The GST Council can recommend a date bringing it under the GST regime without requiring a Constitutional Amendment.\n3. The Constitution explicitly excludes it from the definition of 'Goods and Services Tax'.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. Article 366(12A) defines GST as any tax on supply of goods or services, 'except taxes on the supply of the alcoholic liquor for human consumption.' Therefore, bringing alcohol under GST requires a Constitutional Amendment, not merely a GST Council recommendation. (This is different from petroleum products, which CAN be included via a Council recommendation under Art 279A(5)).","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the Quorum of the GST Council:\n1. The quorum is one-half of the total number of Members.\n2. It requires both the Chairperson (Union FM) and Vice-Chairperson to be present to meet the quorum.\n3. If a meeting is adjourned for want of quorum, the subsequent rescheduled meeting does not require a quorum.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Article 279A(7) states the quorum is one-half of the members. There is no constitutional requirement that specific office bearers (like the Chairperson) must be present specifically for the quorum to be met (though practice dictates the Chairperson or MoS presides). The Constitution doesn't waive quorum for adjourned meetings. Only 1 is correct.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Which of these changes were brought by the 101st Amendment Act regarding the GST Council?\n1. It deleted Article 268A (dealing with Service Tax).\n2. It omitted the entries in the Union List regarding excise duties on medicinal and toilet preparations.\n3. It created Article 246A conferring simultaneous power to Centre and States to make laws regarding GST.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct effects of the 101st Amendment. It deleted Art 268A (service tax), altered the 7th Schedule (removing excise on medicinal preps from Union list as it was subsumed by GST), and most importantly introduced Article 246A giving simultaneous legislative power.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the difference between IGST (Integrated GST) and CGST (Central GST):\n1. IGST is levied on inter-state trade, whereas CGST is levied on intra-state trade.\n2. The GST Council recommends the apportionment of IGST revenues between the Centre and the States.\n3. The proceeds of IGST are entirely retained by the Union Government.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Under Article 269A, IGST is levied and collected by the Union but is apportioned between the Union and the States based on the recommendations of the GST Council. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"According to Article 279A, the GST Council is guided by the need for:\n1. A harmonised structure of goods and services tax.\n2. The development of a harmonised national market for goods and services.\n3. Recommending the abolition of the Finance Commission.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Article 279A(6) literally states: 'While discharging the functions conferred by this article, the Goods and Services Tax Council shall be guided by the need for a harmonised structure of goods and services tax and for the development of a harmonised national market for goods and services.' It has nothing to do with abolishing the Finance Commission.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Principles of Levy' under the GST Council's mandate:\n1. The GST Council recommends the principles of levy (how tax is applied).\n2. It recommends the principles that govern the 'Place of Supply'.\n3. The Place of Supply determines whether a transaction is considered inter-state or intra-state.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. Article 279A(4)(c) mandates the GST Council to recommend the principles of levy, apportionment of IGST under article 269A, and the principles that govern the place of supply (which is the crucial determinant of the jurisdiction receiving the destination-based SGST).","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the phrase 'Every decision of the Goods and Services Tax Council shall be taken at a meeting':\n1. It implies that decisions cannot be taken by mere circulation of files without a formal meeting.\n2. The quorum of one-half members refers to physical or authorized virtual presence.\n3. A defect in the appointment of a State Minister acting as a Member renders the Council's decision unconstitutional.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect because Article 279A(10) establishes a 'savings clause' protecting the Council's proceedings from being invalidated merely due to a defect in the appointment of a person as a Member of the Council. Statements 1 and 2 are correct interpretations of meeting and quorum rules.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Who decides the procedure for the performance of the GST Council's functions?\n1. The Parliament through a statute.\n2. The President through executive rules.\n3. The GST Council determines its own procedure.\nWhich is correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","3 only","1 and 2 only"],"correct_answer":"3 only","explanation":"Article 279A(8) explicitly states: 'The Goods and Services Tax Council shall determine the procedure in the performance of its functions.' This affirms the Council's functional autonomy.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the GST Compensation mechanism (valid till 2022):\n1. It was recommended by the GST Council to compensate states for revenue loss arising out of GST implementation.\n2. The compensation period was constitutionally guaranteed for exactly ten years.\n3. The compensation fund was generated by levying a Compensation Cess on certain luxury and demerit goods.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. Section 18 of the 101st Amendment Act (and the subsequent GST Compensation to States Act) provided for compensation to states for loss of revenue for a period of *five* years (2017 to 2022, later extended slightly for loan repayment, but not a guaranteed 10 years for revenue loss). Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the GST Council Secretariat:\n1. It is funded completely by the states to ensure absolute neutrality.\n2. It has officers taken on deputation from both the Central and State Governments.\n3. The Revenue Secretary of India is its ex-officio Secretary.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect. The Union Cabinet approved the creation of the GST Council Secretariat with its recurring and non-recurring expenses borne by the Central Government. Statements 2 and 3 are correct regarding its staffing and leadership.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the difference in the amendment requirements for the GST Council:\n1. An amendment to Article 279A requires a special majority of Parliament plus ratification by half the State Legislatures.\n2. Changing the composition of the GST Council demands a simple majority in Parliament.\n3. The GST Council itself can amend Article 279A using a 75% weighted vote.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct; being deeply entrenched in federalism, amending Article 279A requires the complex Article 368 process (Special Majority + Half States' ratification). Statements 2 and 3 are incorrect. Parliament cannot change it with a simple majority, and the GST Council absolutely cannot amend the Constitution itself.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"If three state governments totally boycott a GST Council meeting:\n1. The meeting cannot proceed because consensus is broken.\n2. The meeting can proceed as long as the quorum of one-half of total members is met.\n3. The Centre loses its veto power in that specific meeting.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","3 only"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; absolute unanimity is not required, just 75% weighted votes of those *present and voting*. Boycotts don't halt proceedings if the 50% quorum is met (Statement 2). The Centre's weight remains 33.33% of the total votes cast at the meeting regardless of boycotts, meaning its veto (preventing a 75% consensus without it) remains intact (Statement 3 is incorrect).","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Under Article 279A(4)(b), the GST Council can recommend 'exemptions'. This means the Council can decide:\n1. Which goods or services will attract a 0% GST rate.\n2. Which entities (like the UN or specific diplomatic missions) are exempt from GST.\n3. To permanently exempt petroleum from ever being considered for GST.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect because Article 279A(5) clearly mandates that GST *shall* apply to petroleum products from a date recommended by the GST Council. They cannot permanently constitutionally exempt it. Statements 1 and 2 describe the standard exemption powers.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the inclusion of local self-governments (Panchayats and Municipalities) in the GST architecture:\n1. Mayors and Panchayat heads are represented via rotational membership in the GST Council.\n2. Local body taxes (e.g., Octroi, entry tax) were subsumed into GST based on the Council's framework.\n3. The GST Council directly transfers a defined percentage of IGST to local bodies.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; there is no local government representation in the GST Council (only Union and State ministers). Statement 3 is incorrect; IGST is apportioned between Union and States; State Finance Commissions deal with local bodies. Statement 2 is correct (many local entry taxes were subsumed).","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"What is an essential feature of a 'destination-based consumption tax' like GST recommended by the Council?\n1. The tax revenue accrues to the state where the goods are consumed, not where they are manufactured.\n2. It inherently benefits manufacturing states over consuming states.\n3. It requires an integrated mechanism to transfer input tax credit between states.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; destination-based taxes benefit *consuming* states (like UP or Bihar) because the revenue accrues where it is finally consumed, initially to the detriment of major *manufacturing* states (like Gujarat or Tamil Nadu). Statements 1 and 3 are defining features.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the GST Appellate Tribunal (GSTAT) and the GST Council:\n1. The GST Council recommends the creation and structure of GSTAT benches.\n2. GSTAT acts as the secondary forum for dispute resolution if the GST Council fails to resolve a centre-state dispute.\n3. GSTAT hears appeals against orders passed by Appellate Authorities or Revisional Authorities under the GST Act.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. The GSTAT is a tax tribunal handling taxpayer litigations (appeals against tax authorities). It does NOT handle Centre-State constitutional or Council mandate disputes (which the Council's own mechanism or the Supreme Court handles under Art 131). Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"With respect to the principle of 'cooperative federalism' in the GST Council (as per the Mohit Minerals judgment):\n1. The Constitution does not envisage a scenario where the Union can entirely override the states in the GST Council.\n2. The recommendations of the GST Council are the product of a collaborative dialogue, not supreme legislative commands.\n3. Treating the Council's recommendations as legally binding on legislatures would disrupt the fiscal autonomy of Parliament and State Legislatures.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. In the 2022 Mohit Minerals case, Justice D.Y. Chandrachud observed that the GST Council is a space for 'contestation and dialogue'. Making its recommendations binding edicts would destroy the simultaneous fiscal legislative autonomy (Art 246A) granted to states and parliament.","chapter_number":52,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Special Category States' under Article 279A(4)(g):\n1. They enjoy a higher recommended threshold limit for GST registration compared to normal states.\n2. The GST Council can make special provisions for them regarding tax rates and transition periods.\n3. The President can unilaterally alter the list of these states without consulting the GST Council or Parliament.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The list of these specific states is hardcoded into the text of the Constitution (Article 279A(4)(g)). To alter the list of these states, a Constitutional Amendment would be required. Statements 1 and 2 are true (they generally have lower/different thresholds and special dispensations).","chapter_number":52,"level":2},

    # CH52 GST Council L3 (+28)
    {"text":"In the constitutional landscape, Article 246A represents a massive paradigm shift. Why?\n1. It marks a departure from the strict mutually exclusive division of taxation powers found in Lists I and II of the 7th Schedule.\n2. It introduces the concept of 'simultaneous' legislative power over taxing the same transaction (supply).\n3. It subjects Parliament's taxing power entirely to the prior approval of the GST Council.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. While the GST Council recommends, Parliament has the independent constitutional power to enact laws (the recommendations have persuasive, not legally binding, force). Statements 1 and 2 accurately describe the shift from separated silos (e.g., Centre taxing manufacture via excise, states taxing sale via VAT) to simultaneous power over 'supply'.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Analyze the Supreme Court judgment in Union of India vs Mohit Minerals (2022):\n1. The Court held that Article 246A does not contain a 'non-obstante' clause overriding the rest of the Constitution.\n2. The Court asserted that “recommendation” in Article 279A does not mean an enforceable mandate.\n3. The Court highlighted that the 'veto' structure in the Council prevents the Centre from dominating the States absolutely.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect because Article 246A DOES start with a non-obstante clause: \"Notwithstanding anything contained in articles 246 and 254...\". However, Statements 2 and 3 accurately reflect the Court's rationale that recommendations are persuasive and the structural voting rules foster cooperative federalism.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the mechanism of IGST (Integrated GST) under Article 269A:\n1. The Parliament has exclusive power to make laws with respect to GST where the supply of goods or services takes place in the course of inter-state trade.\n2. IGST replaces the erstwhile Central Sales Tax (CST).\n3. IGST is an entirely new tax, distinct from CGST and SGST, the revenue of which is never shared with states.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. IGST is essentially a sum of CGST and SGST levied on inter-state commerce simply to ensure the credit chain remains intact. Its proceeds are apportioned between the Union and the destination State (Art 269A(1)). Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Evaluate the voting mathematics of the GST Council regarding a constitutional crisis:\n1. If all 28 states and 3 UTs (with legislatures) unite against the Centre, they hold ~66.67% of the vote.\n2. Since passing a resolution requires 75%, a united opposition of all states cannot pass a GST Council resolution without the Centre.\n3. This implies the Constitution intentionally embedded a 'Union Veto' within the Council.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. The Centre holds exactly 33.33%. Even if 100% of the State votes (which equal 66.67% of the total) demand a change, they only reach 66.67%, falling short of the 75% required. Thus, the Centre inherently holds a veto block.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the inverse veto power of the States in the GST Council:\n1. The Centre cannot pass a resolution alone, as it only possesses 33.33% of the vote.\n2. To reach the 75% threshold, the Centre needs the support of at least 20 States (assuming all 31 members vote).\n3. Under no circumstance can 12 states block a resolution supported by the Centre and the remaining states.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. All 31 sub-national entities (States+UTs) share the 66.67% equally. Each state has roughly 2.15% of the total vote. To block a resolution, you need >25% of the vote against it. Twelve voting states represent ~25.8% (12 * 2.15%), which is enough to block a resolution. Hence, a minority block of states can effectively veto the Centre.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the difference between the GST Council and the Inter-State Council (ISC):\n1. The ISC is a recommendatory body on broad inter-state issues, while the GST Council is specifically empowered for indirect taxation policy.\n2. Article 263 (ISC) explicitly grants the ISC the power to adjudicate legal disputes, unlike the GST Council.\n3. Both require a 3/4th weighted majority for decision making.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; Article 263 allows ISC to 'inquire into and advise upon' disputes, not adjudicate binding legal decrees. Statement 3 is incorrect; only the GST Council has the constitutional 3/4th weighted voting formula; ISC works mostly by consensus without a rigid weighted vote in the constitution. Only 1 is correct.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Which of the following constitutional constraints did the 101st Amendment place to protect state autonomies?\n1. It allowed states to continue levying taxes on the sale of petroleum products and alcohol.\n2. It explicitly removed 'taxes on the entry of goods into a local area' (Entry 52, State List) to prevent octroi.\n3. It required a bare minimum of 2/3rds of states to ratify the amendment before it became law.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Article 368 requires ratification by not less than *one-half* (1/2) of the States, not 2/3rds. Statements 1 and 2 are factual regarding the adjustments made to Lists I and II to synthesize GST while leaving alcohol and fuel temporarily/permanently out.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Analyze the GST Compensation Act mechanism in a legal context:\n1. It originated from a constitutional guarantee ensuring states a 14% year-on-year revenue growth.\n2. When the compensation cess fell short during the COVID-19 pandemic, the Centre argued it was an 'Act of God', initially refusing to borrow to pay states.\n3. The Supreme Court eventually ruled that the GST Council must dissolve if it fails to pay compensation.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The Constitution (101st Amendment) guaranteed 'compensation for loss of revenue', but the 14% growth figure was formulated in the statutory GST Compensation to States Act, 2017, not the Constitution itself. Statement 3 is historically and legally false. Statement 2 refers to the actual 2020 fiscal dispute where the Centre cited force majeure ('Act of God') before eventually devising a borrowing window.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Place of Supply' rules under IGST:\n1. It is critical because GST is a destination-based consumption tax; the tax revenue must accrue to the final destination.\n2. The GST Council is constitutionally mandated to recommend the principles governing the place of supply.\n3. If a service is performed in State A but billed to a company in State B, the place of supply is always considered State A to protect local revenue.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Complex 'Place of Supply' rules exist precisely for B2B transactions. Usually, for B2B services, the place of supply is the location of the *recipient* (State B), allowing them to claim input tax credit, staying true to destination principle. Statements 1 and 2 describe the fundamental design safely guarded by Art 279A.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Under Article 279A, what differentiates the 'Special Category States' concerning GST?\n1. They are immune from levying CGST altogether.\n2. The GST Council can recommend special provisions for them regarding registration thresholds and composition schemes.\n3. These states were listed based on historical geographic and economic disadvantages.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; they do levy/attract CGST, but their operational burden is eased. Statement 2 is correct (e.g., lower threshold limits like 10/20 lakhs vs 20/40 lakhs for registration). Statement 3 accurately describes why states like the NE group and Himalayan states are given this status under 279A(4)(g).","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the role of the Revenue Secretary in the GST Council:\n1. He/She functions as the ex-officio Secretary to the GST Council.\n2. He/She has the casting vote in case of a tie.\n3. He/She determines the ultimate agenda of the meeting irrespective of the Chairperson.\nWhich is correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"The Revenue Secretary acts as the Secretariat's head (ex-officio Secretary, Statement 1). They have no voting rights, let alone a casting vote (Statement 2 is false). The Chairperson (Union Finance Minister) approves the agenda (Statement 3 is false).","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Analyze the phrase 'subsumed in GST' regarding the GST Council's role:\n1. The Council decided which specific cesses and surcharges previously levied by the Centre would be abolished and merged into the GST rate.\n2. Entertainment tax levied by local bodies (panchayats/municipalities) was subsumed into GST, preventing local dual-taxation.\n3. Basic Customs Duty was subsumed into IGST.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. Entertainment tax levied by states was subsumed, but taxes on entertainment and amusements *levied and collected by a Panchayat, Municipality, or Regional Council* were explicitly kept OUTSIDE GST (Entry 62 of State List was modified to reflect this). Statement 3 is incorrect; Basic Customs Duty (BCD) remains separate, only the Countervailing Duty/SAD portions were replaced by IGST.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Which of the following describes the 'Dual GST' model implemented by India at the recommendation of the GST Council?\n1. Both the Central and State governments simultaneously levy tax across the value chain.\n2. Tax is levied by the Centre on manufacturing and by the States on retail sales.\n3. The Constitution was amended to allow States to tax 'services' which was previously the exclusive domain of the Centre.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; the defining feature of GST is moving AWAY from segmented taxation (Centre = manufacturing/excise, State = retail/sales). Now, both tax the entire value chain simultaneously based on supply. Statement 3 is correct (Article 246A broke the monopoly of Centre over services).","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the GST Council's power to recommend a 'mechanism to adjudicate disputes' (Art 279A(11)):\n1. It completely ousts the original jurisdiction of the Supreme Court under Article 131 for disputes involving Centre and States over GST.\n2. The Council recommended creating a dedicated GST Appellate Tribunal mapped exclusively for constitutional disputes.\n3. The mechanism envisaged is internal to the Council structure, to foster cooperative resolution before litigation.\nWhich are correct?","options":["3 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect. An internal constitutional mechanism cannot oust the fundamental original jurisdiction of the SC under Article 131. Statement 2 is incorrect because the GSTAT resolves taxpayer disputes, not Center-State constitutional disputes over policy. Statement 3 accurately captures the spirit of Art 279A(11) as a first-resort cooperative dispute resolution mechanism.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the Anti-profiteering mechanism under the GST framework:\n1. The GST Council recommended the establishment of the National Anti-profiteering Authority (NAA).\n2. Its purpose was to ensure that the benefits of reduced tax rates or input tax credits were passed on to the final consumer.\n3. The Constitution explicitly mentions the creation of a permanent NAA under Article 279A.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The NAA is a statutory body born from the CGST Act (Section 171), based on the GST Council's recommendation. It is not mentioned in the text of the Constitution. Statements 1 and 2 are historically and factually accurate.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"If a state dissents against a GST Council recommendation to increase the GST rate on automobiles from 18% to 28%:\n1. The state can unilaterally keep its SGST rate corresponding to 18% within its borders.\n2. If the resolution passes with 75% weighted votes, the state is politically and conventionally bound to align its laws, despite retaining legislative power under Art 246A.\n3. The Centre can declare a financial emergency in that state to enforce conformity.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is technically plausible theoretically under Art 246A but practically destroys the GST network (ITC flow), and the Council framework aims to prevent this. Statement 2 is the correct interpretation: the decision binds by convention and systemic pressure. Statement 3 is an extreme misinterpretation of Financial Emergency powers (Art 360).","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Input Tax Credit' (ITC) matching and the GST Council:\n1. The Council's framework restricts seamless ITC across states to encourage local manufacturing.\n2. IGST acts as a bridge, allowing ITC to flow from a selling state to a consuming state.\n3. The Council determines the 'floor rates' but explicitly prevents states from offering ITC on their own state taxes.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","3 only"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is entirely incorrect; GST specifically aims to *create* seamless ITC across states to avoid the 'tax on tax' (cascading) effect. Statement 3 is incorrect; states must provide ITC on SGST to make the chain work. Statement 2 correctly identifies IGST as the clearing house mechanism for cross-border ITC.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Analyze the significance of the 100th and 101st Amendment Acts:\n1. The 100th Amendment dealt with the Land Boundary Agreement with Bangladesh, while the 101st dealt with GST.\n2. Both required ratification by half of the state legislatures under Article 368.\n3. The 101st Amendment was drafted by the First Administrative Reforms Commission.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect because the 100th Amendment (Land Boundary) altered the First Schedule, which does NOT require ratification by half the states under Article 368 (only changes affecting federalism, judiciary, etc., do). The 101st DID require it. Statement 3 is historically false. Only 1 is correct.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the definition of GST under Article 366(12A):\n\"Goods and services tax means any tax on supply of goods, or services or both except taxes on the supply of the alcoholic liquor for human consumption.\"\n1. This implies that 'supply' replaced 'sale', 'manufacture', and 'entry' as the taxable event.\n2. If technological advances create 'intangible digital assets' (like crypto), the broad wording of 'goods or services or both' likely subsumes them into the GST Council's purview without constitutional amendment.\n3. This definition empowers the GST Council to levy a direct tax on the income generated from such supplies.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. GST is purely an indirect tax on the transaction (supply). It does not empower the GST Council or Parliament to reinvent it as a direct tax on income (which remains exclusively under Entry 82, Union List). Statements 1 and 2 accurately reflect the shift in taxable event and the elasticity of the definition.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the constitutional position of 'Tobacco' in relation to the GST Council:\n1. Tobacco products are subjected to GST as recommended by the Council.\n2. Unlike other goods, the Union retains the power to levy Central Excise duty on tobacco products over and above the GST.\n3. It enjoys the same constitutional exclusion as alcoholic liquor for human consumption.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Alcohol is totally excluded from GST. Tobacco, however, is covered by GST. Furthermore, the 101st Amendment explicitly retained the Union's power (Entry 84 of Union List) to levy excise duties on tobacco and tobacco products *in addition* to GST. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Examine the 'Compensation to States' mechanism's constitutional grounding:\n1. It was inserted as Section 18 of the 101st Amendment Act itself, explicitly demanding Parliament to pass a law.\n2. It bypassed the GST Council's authority entirely.\n3. The compensation was fixed on a base year formula assuming a 14% secular growth rate.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. The Constitutional amendment explicitly said that Parliament shall, *on the recommendation of the Goods and Services Tax Council*, provide for compensation to the States. Thus the Council was central to designing it. Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Suppose a new State is carved out in India. Does it automatically get representation in the GST Council?\n1. Yes, the Minister of Finance of the new State automatically becomes a member under Article 279A(2)(c).\n2. A Constitutional Amendment is required to update the list of member states in the GST Council.\n3. The Centre holds discretionary veto over the new state's entry into the Council.\nWhich is correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","3 only","1 and 2 only"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Under Article 279A(2)(c), the Council consists of the 'Minister in charge of Finance or Taxation or any other Minister nominated by *each* State Government'. The moment a state constitutionally exists, its minister has a mandate to be a member. No amendment or central veto blocks this automatic inclusion.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Floor Rates with Bands' provision (Article 279A(4)(e)):\n1. It was heavily debated as states wanted flexibility to increase rates independently during local economic crises.\n2. Widespread use of rate bands would inherently defeat the objective of a 'harmonized national market'.\n3. The GST Council actively uses bands for over 50% of the currently taxable goods to ensure state autonomy.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. While constitutionally allowed to prevent absolute rigidity, the GST Council has rarely, if ever, utilized 'floor rates with bands', strictly enforcing single national rates for items to prevent arbitrage and ensure a unified market. Statements 1 and 2 correctly outline the theory and tension behind the provision.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"With respect to 'Petroleum and Petroleum Products' and the GST Council:\n1. The five specified petroleum products (crude, high-speed diesel, petrol, natural gas, ATF) are legally within the ambit of the GST Constitutional Amendment.\n2. States currently levy VAT/Sales Tax on them, and the Centre levies excise duty.\n3. Bringing them under GST only requires a notified date recommended by the GST Council, not a new constitutional amendment.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. The 101st Amendment included them conceptually but kept them zero-rated/exempt pending a decision by the GST Council (Art 279A(5)). Until that date, the old tax regime (Central Excise and State VAT) continues. No new amendment is needed to transition them.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Analyze the concept of \"Weighted Votes\" in the GST Council:\n1. State A with a population of 200 million has a higher weighted vote than State B with a population of 1 million.\n2. All states, regardless of size or GDP, have identical voting weightage.\n3. The collective votes of all states form exactly two-thirds (66.67%) of the total cast votes.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect. In the GST Council, the principle is 'One State, One Vote' among the states, representing classic federal equality. The vote of UP is equal to the vote of Goa. Their collective total constitutes 66.67% of the entire Council's vote pool.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the role of the Vice-Chairperson of the GST Council:\n1. The Vice-Chairperson is meant to represent the States' interests distinct from the Union Minister who chairs it.\n2. By convention, the Vice-Chairperson is always elected from amongst the opposition-ruled states.\n3. The Constitution makes it mandatory for the Vice-Chairperson to preside if the Chairperson is absent.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; there is no such binding convention. In fact, reaching a consensus on the Vice-Chairperson has often been tricky. Statement 3 is incorrect; the GST Council's procedural rules dictate who presides in absence, the Constitution (Art 279A) only mentions they shall 'choose one amongst themselves'. Only 1 is contextually true regarding the post's federal design.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Evaluate the GST Council's interaction with Article 301 (Freedom of Trade, Commerce and Intercourse):\n1. Prior to GST, differing state VAT rates and entry taxes acted as economic barriers, effectively violating the spirit of Article 301.\n2. The GST Council's mandate to harmonise tax rates ensures removal of border barriers (check posts), realizing the 'One Nation, One Market' vision of Article 301.\n3. The Supreme Court has declared that any GST rate above 18% inherently violates Article 301 by restricting trade.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Tax rates, as long as they are uniform and not discriminatorily designed to create borders, do not violate Article 301. Statements 1 and 2 correctly link the economic philosophy of GST (removing fiscal barriers) with the constitutional aim of Article 301.","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the GST Council's 'Rules of Procedure':\n1. It requires that an absent State Finance Minister cannot send a subordinate official to vote by proxy.\n2. The quorum mandates presence, but the Council has adapted rules to allow presence via video conferencing.\n3. The Rules of Procedure were drafted entirely by the Parliament to ensure uniformity.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect because Article 279A(8) explicitly states that the Goods and Services Tax Council shall determine its *own* procedure. Parliament did not draft it. Statements 1 and 2 reflect the operational rules (ministers must vote; virtual presence usually counts for quorum).","chapter_number":52,"level":3},
]
